{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-109244",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-6I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
